\par
\pard {\b B1.} Is my understanding correct that "FPF % of readings \u8805>= 92% of limit when overflowing" measures compliance with the flow-to-treatment condition of the permit \u8212- that is, whether the works was passing forward the required flow at the times the storm overflow was discharging? If not, please explain what it measures.\par
\par
\pard {\b B2.} In 2025 this record shows 336 consecutive days without good Flow Passed Forward data and 350 without good overflow operation data. Please confirm whether that is correct, explain why the monitoring was unavailable for that period, and state when it was restored.\par
\par
\pard {\b B3.} For 2025 the same record shows compliance of 75%, against 100% in each of 2023 and 2024. What does that 25% represent in terms of actual discharges, and was any of it investigated?\par
\par
\pard {\b B4.} For 2025 this record gives 43.1 hours of overflow operation across 19 operations. The Event Duration Monitoring annual return for the same works and the same year (unique ID SVT01571) gives 305.5 hours across 38 counted spills. Please explain the difference between these two published figures, and state which the Agency regards as the record of what actually happened.\par
\par
\pard {\b B5.} During the period when flow monitoring was unavailable, on what basis was compliance with the permit's flow condition assessed?\par
\par
\pard {\b B6.} Please provide a copy of the current permit for this works, including the flow-to-treatment condition and the storm overflow conditions.\par
\par
\pard\sb240\sa120\keepn\b\fs24 Why I am asking\b0\fs22\par
\par
\pard I am aware that the Agency has already reached a conclusion about the effect of these discharges. Your Catchment Data Explorer classifies water body {\b GB104028052511}, "Erewash from Nethergreen Brook to Gilt Brook", as {\b Poor}, and has done since 2016, having been Moderate in 2013, 2014 and 2015.\par
\par
\pard Of the twenty Reasons for Not Achieving Good recorded against that water body, farming and urban run-off are marked Probable. {\b Two are marked Confirmed, and both are sewage}: "Sewage discharge (continuous)" and "Sewage discharge (intermittent)", each attributed to the Water Industry, waste water treatment sector, affecting ammonia.\par
\par
\pard So the Agency has already confirmed that intermittent sewage discharge is a cause of this river's condition. My questions above are about whether the monitoring that governs those discharges was working.\par
\par
\pard {\b B7.} Given that intermittent sewage discharge is recorded as a {\i Confirmed} reason for this water body not achieving good status, what weight was given to the unavailability of flow monitoring at Milnhay works during 2025 when considering compliance and enforcement?\par
\par
\pard\sb240\sa120\keepn\b\fs28 Part C \u8212- river monitoring in the parish\b0\fs22\par
\par
\pard Your Water Quality Archive holds sampling point {\b MD-45217950}, "RIVER EREWASH UPSTREAM OF MILNHAY SEWAGE TREATMENT WORKS". It is the obvious place to measure what the works does to the river, because the next point downstream (MD-45217250, Shipley Gate) is already sampled monthly.\par
\par
\pard {\b C1.} I can find no results at all from MD-45217950 since 2015. Is that correct? If sampling there has ceased, when and why, and is there any plan to reinstate it?\par
\par
\pard {\b C2.} Downstream at Bailey Brook, Milnhay Road (MD-45691150), ammoniacal nitrogen averaged about 0.10 mg/l each year from 2015 to 2021. In 2022 the annual mean was 0.65 mg/l with a peak sample of 3.50 mg/l, and in 2025 the annual mean was 0.81 mg/l with a peak of 2.60 mg/l. Dissolved oxygen at the same point was at its lowest of the record in 2025. Were either of those years investigated, and if so what was found?\par
\par
\pard\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brsp20 \par\pard\par
\par
\pard I am not alleging a breach of any kind in any of these matters. I am asking because the published data does not allow me to answer these questions myself, and I think it should.\par
\par
\pard I have set out the working behind all three parts, with every source linked, at https://sewage.beardedbuddy.com/ The River Erewash runs the length of this parish and I would like to understand what is permitted to discharge into it and how that is monitored.\par
\par
\pard I would be grateful for a response within the statutory 20 working days.\par
\par
\pard Yours faithfully,\par
\par
\pard {\b [your name]} [your address] [your email]\par
\par
\pard\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brsp20 \par\pard\par
\par
\pard\sb240\sa120\keepn\b\fs28 What to expect, and what to do with it\b0\fs22\par
\par
\pard {\b Part A.} The likeliest answer is a legacy permit for an asset long gone. If so, ask when it will be revoked and how many other dormant sewage permits sit on the register for this catchment \u8212- a live permit for a demolished asset is its own problem. If instead it turns out to be monitored under a reference you could not find, that is a data quality fault in a national dataset, worth reporting and worth telling Top of the Poops about.\par
\par
\pard {\b Part C} is the quiet one, and possibly the most useful. If the upstream point really has recorded nothing since 2015, then nobody \u8212- including the Agency \u8212- can measure what the works does to the river, and that is a straightforward thing to ask them to fix.\par
\par
\pard {\b Part B is the strongest.} B4 is the question to watch: two of the Agency's own published records disagree about the same works in the same year by a factor of seven. There is probably a sound technical reason \u8212- most likely that the 43.1 hours only covers the fraction of the year the instrument was working \u8212- but they should say so, on the record. And if the flow monitoring really was down for 336 days, then for most of 2025 nobody could demonstrate that the works met the condition that makes its spilling lawful.\par
\par
\pard {\b If they miss the deadline}, ask for an internal review, then complain to the Information Commissioner. Both free.\par
\par
\pard {\b Do not run ahead of the answers.} The strength here is that every question is specific, sourced and answerable, and that you have said plainly you are not accusing anyone. That is what makes it hard to fob off.\par
\par
\par
\pard\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brsp20 \par\pard\par
\par
\pard\sb240\sa120\keepn\b\fs34 What came back, and the follow-up\b0\fs22\par
\par
\pard Severn Trent's EIR team answered Parts A and B, with reference {\b EIR1416}, and attached the Cornwood Meadows consent (filed here as {\f1 docs/cornwood-meadows-consent-1983.pdf}). They said they were answering only the parts that concern them. {\b The Environment Agency has not replied yet.} The letter went on 2 September, so its 20 working days ran out on {\b 30 September}.\par
\par
\pard The follow-up drafted earlier (questions D1 to D4) is replaced by what follows. Their answer, and the two permits it led to, settled D1 and D2 and changed what is worth asking.\par
\par
\pard\sb240\sa120\keepn\b\fs28 What their answer says\b0\fs22\par
\par
\pard {\b Part A, Cornwood Meadows.} Severn Trent say the asset is "not classified as a storm overflow", so it is outside Event Duration Monitoring. They are "currently undertaking further investigations to confirm the current status and presence of the pumping station asset". In other words, they do not know whether it is still there. They hold no variations to the permit. A4 (hours discharged) was not answered, and the first half of A1 was answered only by implication.\par
\par
\pard The consent they sent explains the classification. It was issued by the Severn-Trent Water Authority on 18 November 1983 under the Rivers (Prevention of Pollution) Acts, as consent WQ/7/2188. It permits foul sewage from the pumping station, or surface water from the development's sewers, and limits the discharge from the pumping station to "an overflow from the sump when the pumping station ceases to operate due to circumstances outside the control of the Authority and it is not reasonably practicable to dispose of the sewage otherwise". {\b It is an emergency overflow, not a storm overflow.} The register agrees: the permit's effluent type there is "Sewage in an Emergency". The PDF never shows the number T/61/09188/O. The date and site match, though, so it is almost certainly the same consent.\par
\par
\pard {\b Part B, Milnhay.}\par
\par
\pard\li360\fi-180 \'95  {\b B1.} Confirmed: the 92% measure is one of the Agency's tests of compliance\par\pard
\pard with the flow to full treatment requirement.\par
\pard\li360\fi-180 \'95  {\b B2.} Milnhay was demolished as a treatment works and has run as a pumping\par\pard
\pard station since {\b December 2024}, pumping untreated flows to Newthorpe. "Good" data was lost "while the capital scheme was still being undertaken during 2025". Monitoring resumed after a new monitor was installed and certified "later in the reporting year". {\b No date was given}, although one was asked for.\par
\pard\li360\fi-180 \'95  {\b B3.} The 75% means 75% of qualifying overflow events happened while flow\par\pard
\pard passed forward was at least 92% of the permitted figure. {\b The rest is one event in December 2025}, caused by "an issue associated with pump commissioning", when the site overflowed without passing forward the required flow.\par
\pard\li360\fi-180 \'95  {\b B4.} The two figures measure different things. The 43.1 hours on the Water\par\pard
\pard Quality Archive are spills {\i into} the storm tank, and the monitor ran for only part of the year. The 305.5 hours in the EDM return are spills {\i out of} the tank into the river. They also count events differently. Severn Trent stand by the EDM return as accurate for spills to the environment. {\b This is a sound explanation and should be accepted.}\par
\pard\li360\fi-180 \'95  {\b B5.} Other site metering "may be used" when the certified meter is down. They\par\pard
\pard did not say whether it was used at Milnhay, or what it showed.\par
\pard\li360\fi-180 \'95  {\b B6.} They pointed to the new permit.\par\pard
\par
\pard\sb240\sa120\keepn\b\fs28 What the two permits add\b0\fs22\par
\par
\pard I read both permits from the public register. The new one is EPR/MB3043KT (https://environment.data.gov.uk/public-register/water-discharges/registration/EPR-MB3043KT-001), and the old one is T/61/45098/R version 8 (https://environment.data.gov.uk/public-register/water-discharges/registration/MI-T-61-45098-R-008).\par
\par
\pard {\b Sixteen months without a permit matching the site.} Severn Trent's application for the new permit was duly made on {\b 12 December 2024}, the month the site stopped treating. The permit was issued on {\b 20 April 2026}. In between, the only permit was the 2022 treatment-works permit. It authorised final effluent and set an overflow setting of 258 l/s and 2,277 m\u179? of storage. The new permit sets 325 l/s and 7,000 m\u179?. {\b The register gives the old permit a revocation date of 23 October 2026}, almost two years after the works closed.\par
\par
\pard {\b Tests the 2025 record fails, in a permit that did not yet apply.} The new permit's flow monitoring conditions are tests the 2025 record fails badly:\par
\par
\pard\li360\fi-180 \'95  at least 95% of flow readings taken while the overflow operates must be at or\par\pard
\pard above 92% of the limit (condition 3.1.2). 2025 scored 75%;\par
\pard\li360\fi-180 \'95  no more than 14 consecutive days without good flow data, or without good\par\pard
\pard overflow data (condition 3.2.7). 2025 had 336 and 350.\par
\par
\pard The old permit contains neither test for the storm overflow. Its 14-day and 37-day data rules (condition 3.3.10) apply to the final effluent's daily volumes, which no longer existed. So it is possible that {\b nothing in the permit in force was breached by 2025's missing data}, simply because the permit no longer described the site. That is worth confirming rather than assuming.\par
\par
\pard {\b Monitoring due since 2021\u8211-22.} The new permit's introductory note says the storm-storage overflow monitor and MCERTS flow-passed-forward monitor were agreed for delivery by 31 March 2022 and 30 September 2021. The 2023 and 2024 data suggests they were. Severn Trent have now said they were replaced in 2025.\par
\par
\pard {\b A new emergency overflow.} The new permit adds activity A2, an emergency overflow for power failure, pump breakdown, rising main failure or a downstream blockage. It discharges at {\b the same grid reference as the settled storm outfall} (SK 45722 46365). The permit requires event duration monitoring on the storm discharge (A1) only. Nothing in it says how a spill from the emergency overflow would be told apart from a storm spill, or recorded at all.\par
\par
\pard {\b The Event Duration Monitor is due to be replaced.} Table S3.1 sets 15-minute monitoring "until monitor is replaced", then 2-minute.\par
\par
\pard {\b River monitoring is promised.} The introductory note says Severn Trent have confirmed sonde water quality monitoring as part of an AMP8 river quality driver, shared with the Agency, and that "if the monitoring does show negative impacts, steps must be taken to mitigate them". That bears directly on Part C, the upstream sampling point that has recorded nothing since 2015.\par
\par
\pard {\b The screen was misdescribed.} The note also admits that an earlier variation wrongly said all flows were screened, because the screen has a bypass.\par
\par
\pard\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brsp20 \par\pard\par
\par
\pard\sb240\sa120\keepn\b\fs28 Letter 1 \u8212- follow-up to Severn Trent\b0\fs22\par
\par
\pard {\b Send to:} reply to the EIR team's email, keeping reference EIR1416 in the subject, so it joins the existing request.\par
\par
\pard {\b Subject:} EIR1416 \u8212- follow-up: Cornwood Meadows PS and Heanor-Milnhay TPS\par
\par
\pard\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brsp20 \par\pard\par
\par
\pard Dear Severn Trent EIR Team,\par
\par
\pard {\b This is a request for information under the Environmental Information Regulations 2004}, following your response to EIR1416.\par
\par
\pard Thank you for that response, and for the copy of the Cornwood Meadows consent. Your explanation of the difference between the Storm Discharge Monitoring and Event Duration Monitoring figures for Milnhay answers my question B4, and I accept it. Several points need following up. Where a question repeats one I asked before, that is because the response did not give the information requested.\par
\par
\pard\sb240\sa120\keepn\b\fs24 Part E \u8212- Cornwood Meadows pumping station (T/61/09188/O)\b0\fs22\par
\par
\pard The consent you supplied (WQ/7/2188, 18 November 1983) limits the discharge to "an overflow from the sump when the pumping station ceases to operate due to circumstances outside the control of the Authority".\par
\par
\pard {\b E1.} Please confirm that consent WQ/7/2188 is the permit now registered as T/61/09188/O.\par
\par
\pard {\b E2.} You say you are investigating whether the pumping station still exists. When did that investigation begin, when do you expect it to finish, and what has it found so far?\par
\par
\pard {\b E3.} If the pumping station exists:\par
\par
\pard (a) Is it fitted with telemetry that records pump failure, power failure or a high sump level?\par
\par
\pard (b) In each of the last five calendar years, on how many occasions was it inoperative, and on how many of those did sewage leave the sump by the emergency outlet? Please give the date and duration of each, and whether it was reported to the Environment Agency.\par
\par
\pard (c) Is any monitoring fitted that would detect a discharge from the emergency outlet? If not, how would you know that one had happened?\par
\par
\pard {\b E4.} If it does not exist, will you apply for the permit to be revoked, and when? What now happens to the sewage from the Cornwood Meadows development, and under which permit is any overflow from that system made?\par
\par
\pard {\b E5.} The consent refers to Drawings WQ/7/2188/1 and WQ/7/2188/2, and its first condition appears to describe the outlet as marked "SURFACE WATER OUTFALL". Please provide both drawings, or any other record of where the outlet is.\par
\par
\pard\sb240\sa120\keepn\b\fs24 Part F \u8212- Heanor-Milnhay terminal pumping station\b0\fs22\par
\par
\pard {\b F1.} (Repeating B2.) On what date was the replacement flow passed forward monitor certified under MCERTS? On what date did "good" data resume, for flow passed forward and for overflow operation into storm storage?\par
\par
\pard {\b F2.} (Repeating B5.) You say other metering "may be used" when the certified meter is unavailable. During 2025, was any other metering used at Milnhay to assess whether the required flow was being passed forward? If so, what meter was it, and what did it show for the periods when the storm overflow was operating?\par
\par
\pard {\b F3.} The overflow event in December 2025 that you attribute to pump commissioning:\par
\par
\pard (a) On what date did it happen, and for how long?\par
\par
\pard (b) Did it result in a discharge from the storm tanks to the River Erewash, or only in flow entering the storm tanks?\par
\par
\pard (c) Was it reported to the Environment Agency, and if so when?\par
\par
\pard (d) You state that Milnhay has operated as a pumping station since December 2024. Why were pumps still being commissioned a year later?\par
\par
\pard {\b F4.} Between December 2024 and 20 April 2026 the only permit for the site was T/61/45098/R, with an overflow setting of 258 l/s and 2,277 m\u179? of storage. Over that period:\par
\par
\pard (a) To what overflow setting were the storm tanks operated? On what date was the 325 l/s now required by EPR/MB3043KT first passed forward?\par
\par
\pard (b) On what date did the 7,000 m\u179? of storage come into use?\par
\par
\pard {\b F5.} EPR/MB3043KT authorises a new emergency overflow (activity A2) at the same grid reference as the settled storm outfall:\par
\par
\pard (a) When was it commissioned, and has it discharged? If so, when and for how long?\par
\par
\pard (b) Is it monitored? If it discharges, would the Event Duration Monitor for SVT01571 record that as a storm spill, or would it be recorded separately?\par
\par
\pard {\b F6.} Table S3.1 of EPR/MB3043KT requires the settled storm Event Duration Monitor to move from 15-minute to 2-minute monitoring once it is replaced. When will it be replaced?\par
\par
\pard {\b F7.} The permit's introductory note says you have confirmed sonde water quality monitoring as part of an AMP8 river quality driver. Where will the sondes be on the River Erewash, when will they be installed, and will their data be published?\par
\par
\pard {\b F8.} The storm tanks were enlarged from 2,277 m\u179? to 7,000 m\u179?. In 2025, the first full year after the work, they discharged for 305.5 hours. By 1 September 2026 they had discharged for 266 hours. What spill frequency was the enlargement designed to achieve? Do you consider that it has achieved it, and if not, what further work is planned and when?\par
\par
\pard\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brsp20 \par\pard\par
\par
\pard I am grateful for the clear answer on B4. As before, I am not alleging a breach. I am trying to understand what is permitted to reach the Erewash in this parish, and how anyone would know.\par
\par
\pard Yours faithfully,\par
\par
\pard {\b [your name]} [your address] [your email]\par
\par
\pard\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brsp20 \par\pard\par
\par
\pard\sb240\sa120\keepn\b\fs28 Letter 2 \u8212- to the Environment Agency\b0\fs22\par
\par
\pard {\b Send:} now, as a reply to the original request so it keeps its reference. If the Agency's answer arrives first, cut the opening paragraph and send the rest.\par
\par
\pard {\b Subject:} EIR request \u8212- permit T/61/09188/O, Milnhay (Heanor), River Erewash \u8212- response outstanding, and further questions\par
\par
\pard\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brsp20 \par\pard\par
\par
\pard Dear Environment Agency,\par
\par
\pard {\b This is a request for information under the Environmental Information Regulations 2004}, further to my request of 2 September 2026 about permit T/61/09188/O, flow monitoring at Milnhay (Heanor), and river sampling on the Erewash.\par
\par
\pard I have not yet had your response, which was due by 30 September. Severn Trent have answered Parts A and B, and said they were dealing only with the matters relating to them, so the following still need {\b your} answer: A1, B1 to B7, and C1 and C2. Please treat this letter as a reminder of those, and as a further request for the following.\par
\par
\pard\sb240\sa120\keepn\b\fs24 Part G \u8212- permits at Milnhay\b0\fs22\par
\par
\pard The site's new permit, EPR/MB3043KT, was applied for on 12 December 2024 and issued on 20 April 2026. The treatment works permit, T/61/45098/R, is shown on your register with a revocation date of 23 October 2026.\par
\par
\pard {\b G1.} Between December 2024, when Milnhay stopped treating sewage, and 20 April 2026, which permit did the Agency regard as governing the storm tanks at Milnhay? Against which overflow setting and storage volume was compliance assessed?\par
\par
\pard {\b G2.} Why did the determination of EPR/MB3043KT take sixteen months? Why does T/61/45098/R remain in force until 23 October 2026?\par
\par
\pard {\b G3.} EPR/MB3043KT requires at least 95% of flow readings during overflow to be at or above 92% of the limit, and no more than 14 consecutive days without good data. In 2025 the published figures were 75%, and 336 and 350 days. The permit in force in 2025 seems to contain no equivalent condition for the storm overflow. Did any condition apply to flow monitoring at Milnhay in 2025? If so, which, and how did the Agency assess compliance with it?\par
\par
\pard {\b G4.} Severn Trent attribute an overflow in December 2025, made without the required flow being passed forward, to pump commissioning. Was this reported to the Agency? Was it assessed, and with what outcome?\par
\par
\pard\sb240\sa120\keepn\b\fs24 Part H \u8212- Cornwood Meadows\b0\fs22\par
\par
\pard Severn Trent have told me that they are investigating whether the Cornwood Meadows pumping station still exists.\par
\par
\pard {\b H1.} Was the Agency aware of that before this request? What will it do about a permit whose asset the holder cannot confirm exists?\par
\par
\pard {\b H2.} How many other permits in the Erewash catchment have effluent type "Sewage in an Emergency", are held by Severn Trent, and were issued before 1990? Has the existence of their assets been checked?\par
\par
\pard\sb240\sa120\keepn\b\fs24 Part J \u8212- river monitoring (adds to Part C)\b0\fs22\par
\par
\pard The introductory note to EPR/MB3043KT records that Severn Trent will carry out sonde water quality monitoring under an AMP8 river quality driver, with the data shared with the Agency's Analysis and Reporting Team.\par
\par
\pard {\b J1.} Will that data be published, for example in the Water Quality Archive? Where will the sondes be, and from when?\par
\par
\pard {\b J2.} Is it intended to take the place of the Agency's own sampling upstream of Milnhay (MD-45217950)? If not, will that sampling be resumed?\par
\par
\pard\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brsp20 \par\pard\par
\par
\pard Yours faithfully,\par
\par
\pard {\b [your name]} [your address] [your email]\par
\par
\pard\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brsp20 \par\pard\par
\par
\pard\sb240\sa120\keepn\b\fs28 What to expect, and what to do with it\b0\fs22\par
\par
\pard {\b Do not ask for an internal review of EIR1416 yet.} Letter 1 asks again for the three things the response left out (B2's date, B5's substance, A4). That fixes them on the record. If the second answer still leaves them out, ask for an internal review. {\b The 40 working days for that run from receipt of the first response.} Note the date it arrived, so the window is not missed while waiting.\par
\par
\pard {\b The Agency is the one to chase.} If no answer arrives within a week of Letter 2, ask for an internal review on the ground that the deadline has passed. If that fails too, complain to the Information Commissioner.\par
\par
\pard {\b Questions to watch:}\par
\par
\pard\li360\fi-180 \'95  {\b E3(b) and E3(c).} If the pumping station exists and has no telemetry, nobody\par\pard
\pard can say whether the emergency outlet has ever been used.\par
\pard\li360\fi-180 \'95  {\b G1 and G3.} They ask whether 2025 was regulated at all. If the answer is that\par\pard
\pard no condition applied, the site page should say so plainly. It is a fact about how the permit lagged the works, not an accusation.\par
\pard\li360\fi-180 \'95  {\b F5(b).} If an emergency spill would be counted as a storm spill, or not\par\pard
\pard counted at all, it affects how every future figure for this outfall is read.\par
\pard\li360\fi-180 \'95  {\b F7 and J1.} Sondes give a continuous record of what the river is doing. If\par\pard
\pard the data is published, it is the strongest source this site could have.\par
\par
\pard {\b Do not run ahead of the answers.} B4 was answered well and was conceded in Letter 1. Being seen to accept a good answer is what makes the other questions hard to dismiss.\par
\par
}
